---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import InstallCommands from "../components/InstallCommands.astro";

const repo = "https://github.com/zhiganov/impact-to-funding";
const helps = [
  "explore where your skills could be useful,",
  "develop shared work with others,",
  "learn what difference the work makes,",
  "explain what would help it continue.",
];
const publishes = [
  ["Priorities", "the problems, communities and kinds of work it wants to support, and what it excludes."],
  ["Eligibility", "whether individuals, informal groups, organisations or fiscally sponsored projects can apply, and where."],
  ["Support", "the type, typical amount, duration and eligible costs."],
  ["Availability", "whether an opportunity is open, rolling, invitation-only, forecasted, closed or withdrawn."],
  ["Process", "the official route, required materials, deadlines with time zones and reporting expectations."],
  ["Evidence", "what applicants should show, including whether exploratory work without prior results can be supported."],
  ["Freshness", "who published it, authoritative links, and when it was last reviewed."],
];
---
<Base
  title="ItFtI: from impact to funding, and back"
  description="Two open projects that make the way between useful work and funding easier to find, from both ends: Impact to Funding for builders, Funding to Impact for funders."
  current="home"
>
  <section class="hero grid" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
    <img class="seal" src="/itfti-seal.svg" width="1824" height="1820"
      alt="The ItFtI seal: the letters I, t, F, t, I form the walls of a maze, with an entrance at the bottom and an exit at the top." />
    <div class="copy">
      <h1 id="hero-title">Find the way from impact to funding.</h1>
      <p class="lede">Many projects get lost before they reach support. ItFtI works on both ends of the maze: a skill that helps builders make their case, and a file format that lets funders say what they support.</p>
      <div class="lane rule-solid">
        <h2><a href="/itf">Impact to Funding</a> <span>builders · experimental</span></h2>
        <p>An open-source skill for your AI agent: find where your skills help, build with others, and explain what would help the work continue.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="lane rule-dash">
        <h2><a href="/fti">Funding to Impact</a> <span>funders · draft 0.1</span></h2>
        <p>A file format funders publish on their own website, so people and agents can see what they support before anyone applies. Looking for its first funders.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="act">
        <a class="slab" href="#install">Install ItF</a>
        <span>free and open source</span>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="course maze grid" id="maze" aria-labelledby="maze-title">
    <h2 id="maze-title" class="head">The maze</h2>
    <div class="body">
      <p class="big">Useful work often stalls on the way to support. Builders lose time reading scattered, outdated or vague funding pages. Funders read applications that were never a fit.</p>
      <p>ItFtI starts from both sides. From the builder's side, it helps people describe their work and what would help it continue. From the funder's side, it proposes a plain way to say what they support, before anyone spends time applying.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="course itf grid" id="itf" aria-labelledby="itf-title">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="itf-title">Impact to Funding</h2>
      <p class="tag rule-solid">For builders. Available now as an experimental skill.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p class="big">ItF is a skill for the AI agent you already use. It helps you:</p>
      <ul class="helps">{helps.map((h) => <li>{h}</li>)}</ul>
      <p>Start with your situation. You don't need a theory of change, a project, evidence or a fundraising goal. It's for developers, maintainers and independent builders who already use AI agents; community organisers, small teams and facilitators may find it useful too.</p>
      <h3 id="install">Install</h3>
      <InstallCommands />
      <p>ItF also has an optional MCP server: three read-only tools that let any agent search its reference library of cases and patterns. <a href="/itf#mcp">Set it up</a>.</p>
      <p class="actions">
        <a class="slab-ghost" href="/itf">How it works</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="course loop grid" id="loop" aria-labelledby="loop-title">
    <h2 id="loop-title" class="head">How the two meet</h2>
    <div class="body">
      <ol class="sides">
        <li><h3>Impact → funding</h3><p>A builder works out what their contribution is, what difference it makes so far, and what support would help it continue.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Funding → impact</h3><p>A funder states what it supports, who can apply, how much, by when and with what evidence, in a form people and agents can read.</p></li>
      </ol>
      <p>When both sides speak plainly, fewer people get lost in the maze. ItF is meant to be one reader of what funders publish, not the only one and not a gatekeeper. The two parts can be used separately.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="course fti grid" id="fti" aria-labelledby="fti-title">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="fti-title">Funding to Impact</h2>
      <p class="tag rule-dash">For funders. Draft 0.1, looking for its first funders.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p class="big">Before I apply, can I tell whether this funder supports work like mine, whether I'm eligible, and how to approach them?</p>
      <p>FtI is a file format, <code>fti-manifest.json</code>, that a funder publishes on its own website. It's inspired by <a href="https://llmstxt.org/">llms.txt</a> and built on <a href="https://commongrants.org/">CommonGrants</a>: each opportunity is a CommonGrants record inside the FtI file. In it, a funder explains:</p>
      <dl class="publishes">
        {publishes.map(([k, v]) => (<div><dt>{k}</dt><dd>{v}</dd></div>))}
      </dl>
      <p>Funding interests are not open calls. Past awards don't mean applications are open now. Missing eligibility is unknown, not permission to apply.</p>
      <p>An MCP server reads these files, so agents can find funders and open opportunities with four read-only tools: <code>find_funders</code>, <code>search_opportunities</code>, <code>get_entry</code> and <code>registry_info</code>. ItF is one of its intended readers, not the only one.</p>
      <p class="actions"><a class="slab" href="mailto:hello@citizeninfra.org?subject=Publishing%20with%20Funding%20to%20Impact">Publish with FtI</a><a class="slab-ghost" href="/fti">How FtI works</a></p>
      <p class="fine">Funders: write to <a href="mailto:hello@citizeninfra.org?subject=Publishing%20with%20Funding%20to%20Impact">hello@citizeninfra.org</a> to be among the first to publish.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="course status grid" id="status" aria-labelledby="status-title">
    <h2 id="status-title" class="head">What exists today</h2>
    <div class="body">
      <ul class="ledger">
        <li class="rule-solid"><strong>Impact to Funding</strong> is published and open source. It offers conversation, optional templates, reference material and an optional local MCP server for its reference library. It doesn't find collaborators, provide funding or certify impact. Trials so far were small; we haven't tested how useful it is to people or whether it helps them raise money.</li>
        <li class="rule-dash"><strong>Funding to Impact</strong> is at draft 0.1. The file format has executable schemas and a validator, and the MCP server has four read-only tools. Both run locally and have been tested with fictional data. Importers for funders who don't publish the file are planned. No funder has adopted FtI yet, and there is no hosted service.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="actions"><a class="slab" href={repo}>ItF on GitHub</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero { align-items: center; row-gap: 32px; padding-bottom: clamp(40px, 6vw, 96px); }
  .seal { grid-column: 1 / span 6; width: 100%; height: auto; max-height: min(64svh, 640px); object-fit: contain; }
  .copy { grid-column: 7 / span 6; }
  .lede { font-size: var(--step-1); line-height: 1.4; margin-top: 0.7em; }
  .lane { margin-top: 22px; padding-top: 10px; }
  .lane h2 { font-size: var(--step-1); }
  .lane h2 a { text-decoration: none; }
  .lane h2 a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
  .lane h2 span { font-family: var(--body); font-weight: 600; font-size: max(12px, 0.55em); letter-spacing: 0.12em; text-transform: uppercase; margin-left: 0.5em; white-space: nowrap; }
  .lane p { margin-top: 6px; }
  .act { margin-top: 24px; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; gap: 12px 20px; font-size: 0.9rem; }

  .head { grid-column: 1 / span 4; align-self: start; position: sticky; top: 24px; }
  .body { grid-column: 5 / span 8; display: grid; gap: 1.1em; min-width: 0; }
  .big { font-family: var(--display); font-weight: 500; font-size: var(--step-1); line-height: 1.3; max-width: 34ch; }
  .tag { margin-top: 14px; padding-top: 10px; font-weight: 600; max-width: 26ch; }

  .helps { list-style: none; padding: 0; font-family: var(--display); font-weight: 700; font-size: var(--step-1); line-height: 1.3; }
  .helps li { padding: 0.35em 0; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--red); }
  .hosts { max-width: 560px; }
  .fine { font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .actions { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 14px; margin-top: 0.4em; }

  .sides { list-style: none; padding: 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 0; border: 8px solid var(--red); }
  .sides li { padding: 22px 24px; max-width: none; }
  .sides li + li { border-left: 8px solid var(--red); }
  .sides h3 { margin-bottom: 0.4em; }

  .publishes { display: grid; gap: 0; }
  .publishes div { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 9.5em 1fr; gap: 16px; padding: 0.7em 0; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--red); }
  .publishes dt { font-family: var(--display); font-weight: 700; }
  .publishes dd { margin: 0; max-width: 60ch; }

  .ledger { list-style: none; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: 24px; }
  .ledger li { padding-top: 14px; }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .hero { min-height: 0; }
    .seal { grid-column: 1 / -1; max-width: 460px; justify-self: center; max-height: none; }
    .copy, .head, .body { grid-column: 1 / -1; }
    .head { position: static; }
    .sides { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .sides li + li { border-left: 0; border-top: 8px solid var(--red); }
    .publishes div { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 2px; }
    .lane h2 span { display: block; margin: 6px 0 0; }
  }
  /* Phones: smaller seal, and the install action straight after the lede so it sits in the first screen. */
  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .hero { row-gap: 20px; }
    .seal { max-width: 210px; justify-self: start; }
    .copy { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
    .act { order: 1; margin-top: 18px; }
    .lane { order: 2; }
  }
</style>
